issue_context:
Calling `quirks::set_port()` with invalid prefix characters unset the port (WPT non-compliance)
In browsers (tested on Chrome, Firefox and Safari), doing the following:

```js
const u = new URL("https://example.com:8000");
u.port = "\u00009000";
console.log(u.port);
```

will show port `8000` (unchanged). This is also tested in the WPT suite here: https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/blob/2a64dae4641fbd61bd4257df460e188f425b492e/url/url-setters-stripping.any.js#L85-L91. That's how the bug came to my attention.

Doing the same using:

```rust
let mut url = url::Url::parse("http://example.com:8000").unwrap();
url::quirks::set_port(&mut url, "\u{0000}9000").unwrap();
assert_eq!(url.port(), Some(8000));
```

will panic with `url.port()` returning `None`.

patch:
diff --git a/url/src/quirks.rs b/url/src/quirks.rs
index 391a50d..28ec039 100644
--- a/url/src/quirks.rs
+++ b/url/src/quirks.rs
@@ -248,8 +248,17 @@ pub fn set_port(url: &mut Url, new_port: &str) -> Result<(), ()> {
         if !url.has_host() || url.host() == Some(Host::Domain("")) || scheme == "file" {
             return Err(());
         }
+        // If the input doesn't start with an ASCII digit (after stripping leading whitespace),
+        // don't modify the port and return an error, to match browser behavior.
+        // Empty string is allowed (for clearing the port).
+        // We need to strip leading whitespace because parse_port doesn't handle it.
+        let new_port_trimmed = new_port.trim_start();
+        if !new_port_trimmed.is_empty() && !new_port_trimmed.starts_with(|c: char| c.is_ascii_digit()) {
+            return Err(());
+        }
+        // Pass the trimmed string to parse_port so whitespace doesn't cause issues
         result = Parser::parse_port(
-            Input::new_no_trim(new_port),
+            Input::new_no_trim(new_port_trimmed),
             || default_port(scheme),
             Context::Setter,
         )

issue_context:
Convenience methods to consume response body
It would be nice to provide convenience methods for consuming a response body without storing it. This is a useful feature because if the user drops the response without reading the response body fully, the underlying connection cannot be reused and must be closed prematurely. While totally acceptable, this is not a good way to maintain high throughput since it prevents connection reuse.

The API could be something as simple as:

```rust
impl Body {
    pub fn consume(&mut self) -> io::Result<u64> {
        // ...
    }

    pub async fn consume_async(&mut self) -> io::Result<u64> {
        // ...
    }
}
```

Which would be essentially a convenience around `io::copy(&mut response.body_mut(), &mut io::sink())`.

